% ============================================================================
% frog-life-cycle — each stage of the frog's life cycle as a NUMBER (1-3),
% the position in the ordered sequence (adj-facts-stdlib, BIOLOGY).
% ============================================================================
% A sibling library to the already-shipped `monarch-life-cycle.adj` — the
% SAME plain numbered life-cycle-stage recall shape, applied to a DIFFERENT
% organism. Frogs undergo metamorphosis just like monarch butterflies, but
% through a distinct sequence of named stages. Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? frog_life_stage(tadpole, $N)   % 2
%
% A native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5): each `row` lowers to a ground relation
% `frog_life_stage(stage, order)` carrying the source citation, so the
% lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding query — the engine returns the
% position WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero model calls, and abstains
% on a word that is not one of the three stages the source names. The query
% also runs BACKWARD — bind a number and recall the stage:
%
%     ? frog_life_stage($S, 1)   % egg
%
% The three stages IN ORDER, as a little truth table:
%
%     stage     order   a beginner's cue
%     -------   -----   --------------------------------------------
%     egg       1        laid in water, clumped together as frogspawn
%     tadpole   2        hatches with a tail and gills, lives in water
%     frog      3        fully grown, breathes with lungs, hops on land
%
% Provenance: quoted verbatim from National Geographic Kids UK's "The Frog
% Life Cycle for Kids" page, which names the three stages under numbered
% headings — the ordinal reading is the row order, the same discipline
% `biology/monarch-life-cycle.adj` already established for a source's own
% numbered enumeration:
%
%   egg -> 1: "Stage 1: Extraordinary eggs"
%   tadpole -> 2: "Stage 2: Teeny tadpoles!"
%   frog -> 3: "Stage 3: Fully-grown frog!"
%
% WebFetch-verified TWICE for consistency before writing, both fetches
% returning the SAME three numbered headings verbatim. `trust consensus` —
% National Geographic Kids is a reputable, widely-cited children's science
% publisher, the same tier this stdlib already reserves for other
% National Geographic sources (see `animal-habitat.adj`,
% `rainforest-layer.adj`). The source's own narrative describes legs
% developing DURING the tadpole stage, but gives that transition no
% separate numbered heading, so this table deliberately does NOT invent a
% fourth "froglet" row the source never numbers. A word that is NOT one of
% these three stages — `adult` (the source's own heading says "frog," not
% "adult") — is deliberately NOT a row, so a recall ABSTAINS on it rather
% than inventing a position. (See ../README.md;
% feedback_nothing_human_authored, feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table frog_life_stage {
    columns stage, order

    row (egg, 1)
    row (tadpole, 2)
    row (frog, 3)

    source "Stage 1: Extraordinary eggs"
    locator "https://www.natgeokids.com/uk/discover/science/nature/frog-life-cycle/"
    trust consensus
}
